 - Note: it currently contains spoilers: a spoiler-free implementation was considered, so the human/active player can give to an LLM or such their play summary and get LLM or such suggestions or feedback/analysis, but it would require an extensive rework, so currently this is not supported; therefore, SAS game-summary log should as of now not be treated as a spoiler-free player-advice export. Note 2: depending on `SAS_GAME_SUMMARY_TURN_INTERVAL` value, the last turn may not appear in the log: for example, an autoplay run of 200 turns ends at T200 and thus does not seem to show the T200 data, only up to T190, so autoplay at least 201 turns to show T200 for example if you have this issue. For stronger LLM analysis, also provide compact mod context when possible; for example, the AdvCiv-SAS light source ZIP from [`make_light_source_zip.py`](/LLM_Helpers/README.md#make_light_source_zippy) includes useful source/XML/docs/helper context while staying compact enough to upload and share. See also [README.md (DLL Logging)](/README.md#dll-logging).
 - **BBAI worker log split:** added separate worker and Work Boat / `UNITAI_WORKER_SEA` BBAI log-level defines so ordinary `SAS_BBAI_UNIT_LOG_LEVEL` can focus on non-worker unit behavior such as scrapping, army movement, and combat-unit decisions. Worker and worker-sea logs had become noisy enough to hide those unit decisions, and Work Boats use distinct logic from land workers, so they now have their own category as well.
 - **BBAI Settler log split:** added `SAS_BBAI_SETTLER_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT so Settlers and other units can be inspected independently. This notably covers city-site decisions, Settler movement, scouting, returns, founding, (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6027+) parking reasons, danger context, and city escort pools; FOUND as of now separately controls detailed city-site scoring calculations. This notably lets UNIT investigate other unit behavior without enabling the multi-megabyte first-city Settler traces needed for example for the Berlin, Karakorum, and Cuzco regression tests.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6034+) **BBAI War diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_WAR_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from TEAM so UWAI war-target choice can be inspected without broad team/diplomacy logs. It notably records target utility, limited-vs-total war comparison, naval/preparation context, forced peace, attitude, closeness, city distance, power, selected target, target switches, direct-war adoption, and war-plan lifecycle changes, which should help investigate problematic faraway wars before changing behavior.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6034+) **BBAI War diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_WAR_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from TEAM so UWAI war-target choice can be inspected without broad team/diplomacy logs. It notably records target utility, limited-vs-total war comparison, naval/preparation context, forced peace, attitude, closeness, city distance, power, selected target, target switches, direct-war adoption, war-plan lifecycle changes, and optional high-utility scenario/aspect breakdowns, which should help investigate problematic faraway wars before changing behavior.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6031+) **Great General BBAI diagnostics:** added `SAS_BBAI_GREAT_GENERAL_LOG_LEVEL`, separated from UNIT notably so Military Academy construction, Military Instructor joining, Great General unit attachment, target-city choice, and target-unit choice can be inspected together without enabling broad unit logs. This notably helped isolate a bad France sample where `AI_join` rejected all cities for safety/path reasons, then the plot-danger branch attached a Great General to a low-value Longbowman.
 - **Culture-victory BBAI diagnostics:** added a new dedicated `SAS_BBAI_CULTURE_LOG_LEVEL` notably for diagnosing premature, uneven, or wasteful AI culture investment without enabling broad player/city logs. It records for example culture-stage reasons, current/projected Legendary-city countdowns and rival ranking, competing victory progress, actual culture-slider decisions, per-city weights and production, and culture contributed by production processes and assigned specialists.
