 - **War target selection — distance guard**: added a **distance-based pre-filter** in `Risk::preEvaluate` (WarUtilityAspect.cpp) so the AI **rejects faraway rivals** during war-risk evaluation. This curbs "bait" wars that leave cores under-defended and keeps armies concentrated near relevant fronts. See [KI#61](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#61---seemingly-tremendously-improved-ais-getting-baited-to-attack-faraway-rivals-leaving-their-core-cities-mostly-defenseless-but-and-oftentimes-dying-shortly-after-to-an-opportunistic-rivals-changed-by-adding-a-pre-filter-reject-in-war-risk-assessment-based-on-distance-to-us-in-riskpreevaluate-in-warutilityaspectcpp).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6036+) **War target selection — distance guard** - Update: **Faraway-war Risk reject no longer inflates UWAI war utility: a follow-up BBAI test in save file 450 showed the older AdvCiv-SAS KI#61 hard reject could also fire in the recursive peace scenario. Since UWAI scores war as `war scenario - peace scenario`, this could turn a mediocre war into an absurdly attractive one, e.g. `warScenarioUtility=-220`, `peaceScenarioUtility=-105386`, `finalUtility=105166`. The hard reject now applies only to actual war scenarios; retesting removed all huge positive final-utility artifacts (`positive final: 0`) while preserving the intended negative reject for bad faraway/unreachable war plans. See [KI#183](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#183---fixed-advciv-sas-faraway-war-risk-hard-reject-could-make-mediocre-wars-look-extremely-good-by-penalizing-the-peace-scenario).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6035+) **UWAI target choice avoids faraway wars:** Following [KI#61](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#61---seemingly-tremendously-improved-ais-getting-baited-to-attack-faraway-rivals-leaving-their-core-cities-mostly-defenseless-but-and-oftentimes-dying-shortly-after-to-an-opportunistic-rivals-changed-by-adding-a-pre-filter-reject-in-war-risk-assessment-based-on-distance-to-us-in-riskpreevaluate-in-warutilityaspectcpp)'s related fix, BBAI war-target logging in save file 450 showed the AI could still prepare some avoidable faraway wars: for example, on turn 182, Arabia (Team 0) could ignore closer Holy Rome (Team 6) at distance 5, attitudeValue -2, and targetPowerPercent 64, then prepare against the farther Netherlands (Team 5) at distance 10, attitudeValue +1, and targetPowerPercent 92. That is dangerous because a faraway war can split armies from the core, slow reinforcements, expose cities to opportunistic invasions, or make captures harder to hold. The new guard only optionally promotes a nearby land-war target, not an island/naval target, when it is close enough, weak enough, disliked/neutral enough, and has minimal UWAI drive (i.e. current war-preparation interest); With the current distance threshold, it only steps in when the alternative target is at least 2 tiles farther away; targets at the same distance or closer are left to normal UWAI. This is intentionally a narrow blunder-fix rather than a war-logic overhaul, so UWAI choices do not become too bland or broadly overridden. In the confirming BBAI run, the guard found 32 valid closer targets and blocked 17 farther alternatives, leaving usual UWAI logic to either pick these closer targets or continue normally if no suitable target remained. See [KI#182](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#182---fixedimproved-uwai-war-target-selection-could-fall-through-to-farther-targets-even-when-a-closer-weakdisliked-land-target-was-available).
 - **City evacuation — doomed city auto-retreat (land units only)**: in `CvUnitAI::AI_evacuateCity`, the AI now optionally (default ON, recommended) **evacuates 100% of its land units** from a **doomed city** (i.e. a city that exceeds the existing `AI_EVACUATION_THRESH`-based (default as of now: 138%) attacker/defender strength threshold (note: the threshold is raised for important cities, as it is worth defending them and generally bad to evacuate them)). Fixed an inherited Base AdvCiv percentile inversion that instead gave this important-city protection to low-value cities; see [KI#172](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#172---fixed-base-advciv-bug-inverted-city-value-percentile-gave-intended-important-city-evacuation-protection-to-low-value-cities). Previously it often retreated only a tiny fraction (e.g., 2 of 11 defenders vs a 22+ stack, whereas now all 11 defenders would evacuate if the city is deemed doomed). This change might prolong wars/game length, but the intent is a **more competitive, less self-sabotaging** AI. See [KI#62](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#62---extremely-betterstronger-ai-almost-not-evacuating-at-all-doomed-cities-2-swordsmen-in-an-11-unit-defending-stack-vs-a-22-attacking-unit-stack-fixed-by-always-and-100-evacuating-city-doomed-city-regardless-of-land-unit-type-in-cvunitaiai_evacuatecity).
 - **Minimum Stack size before war**: Added a minimum stack unit count requirement to prevent the stack from being marked as "ready" until it has enough units: no `MOVE_DECLARE_WAR` flag, no premature advance toward the city, and AI keeps building units until the stack is big enough. Helps fix the **issue of "back and forth" war declarations with immediate retreat without city attack**: low-numbered unit stacks were especially sensible to power swings (city defenses, one more enemy units skews win chance, etc.). Change in `CvUnitAI::AI_attackCityMove`. See [KI#104](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#104---tremendously-improved-base-advciv-issue-of-the-weird-back-and-forth-of-declaring-war-moving-a-stack-then-withdrawing-without-attacking-which-is-extremely-inefficient).

